{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgE6Hrwizhat8L7ZUT5e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, and the self-check prints **OK**: the file loads, the log-probability is finite, the data contract holds, and a short fit gives a finite ELPD-LOO.\n\n**Model:** `attention_lapse_rule_built_tally_switch`, a refinement of the incumbent `rule_built_tally_switch_triplet`.\n\n**What I kept:** everything in the incumbent. That is the tally-span ideal with person-specific, length-scaled sensitivity; closeness to an ideal switching rate with a person-specific weight; the reward for variety of three-flip chunks; the penalty on rule-built sequences; and each person's left/right lean.\n\n**The one change:** each person now has their own attention-lapse rate. On that share of trials they don't look at the sequences and pick a side at random, so `p_left = ε_i/2 + (1−ε_i)·sigmoid(logit)`. The lapse rates come from a non-centred population distribution with 400 slots, so the model can still predict new participants. This targets the critique's only discrepancy that survives the false-discovery correction (`participant_majority_agreement_sd`, q = 0.048): people differ in how consistently they side with the majority more than the incumbent allows.\n\n**Caveats from the check:**\n- The short fit's ELPD-LOO was −3393.8, against the incumbent's −3413.6. That is only a rough sign, not a real comparison. The check used 1 chain with 100 draws, so R-hat couldn't be computed and convergence wasn't verified.\n- That short fit also rated PSIS-LOO unreliable: 8% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, well over the 1% tolerance. The full admission fit may show the same problem.\n- The smoke fit took about 3.5 minutes. Admission's full fit has a 30-minute limit per sampling run, which I haven't tested against.\n- The check doesn't test novelty against the incumbent. A lapse pulls predictions toward 50/50, so I expect 